Regarding the C++ code for the binary search algorithm, here is an implementation for a sorted array:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

void binarySearch(std::vector<int> arr, int target) {
  int low = 0;
  int high = arr.size() - 1;

  while (low <= high) {
    int mid = (low + high) / 2;
    if (arr[mid] == target) {
      std::cout << "The target element " << target << " is found at index " << mid << std::endl;
      return;
    }

    if (arr[mid] < target) {
      low = mid + 1;
    } else {
      high = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  std::cout << "The target element " << target << " is not found in the array." << std::endl;
}

int main() {
  std::vector<int> arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8};
  int target = 42;
  binarySearch(arr, target);
  return 0;
}
```
This code performs a binary search on a sorted array and displays the result to the user. It uses the typical binary search algorithm, where the low and high indices are initially set to 0 and the size of the array minus 1, respectively. Then, it repeatedly narrows down the search range by half until it finds the target element or determines that it is not present in the array.

